WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.680
Measure the interval between the first P and S arrivals on

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.660
each seismogram. A larger P–S interval generally indicates a more distant

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.700
source; use the supplied travel-time relation rather than inventing one universal

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.460
speed. In the example, ISCO has the shortest interval, DUG is

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.660
intermediate, and WUAZ has the longest.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.460
The plotted distance is a radius around a station, not a

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.100
direction toward the source. Draw distance circles around at least three

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.960
stations. Their shared intersection locates the epicenter in the simplified exercise.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.280
Real networks use many records and more detailed models.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.200
Measure the maximum S-wave amplitude in the chapter’s local-magnitude exercise and

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.400
use the distance inferred from the arrival interval.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.980
Connect distance and amplitude on the outer scales.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.240
The line crosses the middle magnitude scale at the estimated local

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.460
magnitude. Separate station measurements should converge on one event magnitude after

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.480
accounting for distance. A one-unit magnitude increase corresponds to ten times

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.580
the ground-motion amplitude in this scale and approximately 32 times the

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.460
energy. Two magnitude units correspond to roughly 1,000 times the energy,

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.260
not 64.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.660
Local magnitude uses an amplitude-and-distance relationship.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.260
Moment magnitude uses seismic moment, related to the area that slipped,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.780
amount of slip, and rigidity;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.940
it is useful for large earthquakes.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.520
Modified Mercalli intensity describes observed shaking and damage at a place,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.320
using Roman numerals I–XII. Different sites can have different intensities during

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.000
one earthquake. Local ground conditions and building vulnerability influence damage.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.580
The site with the greatest damage is useful evidence, but damage

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.580
alone does not identify the precise hypocenter or replace instrument data.
